WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.394
You open Paint to draw. You
never open the operating system.

2
00:00:08.614 --> 00:00:13.815
Both are software. One does a job
for you; the other runs the machine.

3
00:00:14.315 --> 00:00:17.659
An application is a program
you open to do a job.

4
00:00:17.879 --> 00:00:22.105
Paint to draw. A browser to
read pages. A game to play.

5
00:00:22.325 --> 00:00:26.179
A writing program to write.
All four are applications.

6
00:00:26.679 --> 00:00:30.952
Systems software runs the computer
itself. You never open it.

7
00:00:31.172 --> 00:00:34.469
The operating system starts
when you press power.

8
00:00:34.689 --> 00:00:37.383
It runs the screen, the
keyboard and the mouse.

9
00:00:37.603 --> 00:00:42.293
It keeps your files in folders and
shares the machine between applications.

10
00:00:42.513 --> 00:00:45.857
A driver lets it talk to
the printer or the camera.

11
00:00:46.077 --> 00:00:49.745
It is not just the desktop
picture. It runs everything else.

12
00:00:50.245 --> 00:00:53.775
Input devices record
data. The data goes in.

13
00:00:53.995 --> 00:00:57.013
A keyboard records the
letters a person types.

14
00:00:57.233 --> 00:01:01.227
A camera records a picture.
A microphone records sound.

15
00:01:01.447 --> 00:01:05.302
A temperature sensor records
how warm it is, all by itself.

16
00:01:05.522 --> 00:01:09.098
A motion sensor records that
something moved in front of it.

17
00:01:09.318 --> 00:01:11.965
A data logger is a sensor with a memory.

18
00:01:12.185 --> 00:01:16.179
It records a reading every minute
all night, and keeps them all.

19
00:01:16.679 --> 00:01:21.090
Output devices communicate
information. It comes out, to you.

20
00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:25.118
The screen shows the weather:
today it is 22 degrees.

21
00:01:25.338 --> 00:01:29.936
The speaker says battery low. A
voice note is information as sound.

22
00:01:30.156 --> 00:01:33.593
The printer puts the class
list on paper, for you to keep.

23
00:01:33.813 --> 00:01:39.803
A red light warns you the fridge door is
open: one light, one piece of information.

24
00:01:40.303 --> 00:01:44.715
File size is measured in bytes.
A kilobyte is about a thousand.

25
00:01:44.935 --> 00:01:49.440
A megabyte is about a million. A
gigabyte is a thousand million.

26
00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:54.118
A ten-page story is about twenty
kilobytes. Letters are tiny.

27
00:01:54.338 --> 00:01:58.796
A photo is about three megabytes:
millions of coloured dots.

28
00:01:59.016 --> 00:02:03.660
A song is about five megabytes.
A film is about two gigabytes.

29
00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:07.874
A game can be a few megabytes,
or as much as fifty gigabytes.

30
00:02:08.374 --> 00:02:14.365
Now put them in order, smallest
first: text, image, audio, video.

31
00:02:14.585 --> 00:02:17.743
You might think a long story
is bigger than a photo.

32
00:02:17.963 --> 00:02:22.189
It is not. A whole book of
text is smaller than one photo.

33
00:02:22.409 --> 00:02:26.263
The file format decides the
size, not the number of pages.

34
00:02:26.763 --> 00:02:31.314
Applications do jobs for you.
Systems software runs the machine.

35
00:02:31.534 --> 00:02:36.596
Input devices record data. Output
devices communicate information.

36
00:02:36.816 --> 00:02:42.064
Text, image, audio, video, games:
files come in very different sizes.
